<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Isles</h1><p class="st-meta">1079 CE – 1266 CE · capital Castletown (Rushen)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Norse-Gaelic sea kingdom of Man and the Hebrides, ruled from the Isle of Man by the Crovan dynasty under Norwegian overlordship.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godred-crovan">Godred Crovan</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norse-gaels">Norse-Gael</a> who had fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stamford-bridge">Stamford Bridge</a> in 1066, seized the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/isle-of-man">Isle of Man</a> in 1079 after a battle at Sky Hill. His descendants ruled Man and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a>, the Sudreys, as a kingdom under the nominal overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom was a maritime power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and island fortresses, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castle-rushen">Castle Rushen</a> and the church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rushen-abbey">Rushen Abbey</a>. Man's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tynwald">Tynwald</a> assembly descends from its Norse institutions. Its kings intervened in Irish and Scottish affairs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerled">Somerled's</a> revolt in 1156 took the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a>, leaving the Crovan line with Man, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-lewis">Lewis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-skye">Skye</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-largs">Battle of Largs</a> in 1263, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> ceded Man and the Isles to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-perth">Treaty of Perth</a> in 1266, the year after the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-olafsson">Magnus Olafsson</a>, died. Man later came under English rule as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mann">Lordship of Mann</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-the-isles.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Isles" width="320" height="336"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Mann_and_the_Isles-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1079 CE – 1266 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Castletown (Rushen)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Godred Crovan, Olaf I, Magnus Olafsson</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse, Gaelic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Mann and the Isles, Sudreyjar, Kingdom of the Sudreys</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1172&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-isles" aria-label="Kingdom of the Isles on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4577 323 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of the Isles lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4897 579L5225 425M4897 579L4861 599M4897 579L4892 500M4897 579L4897 579"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0M4861 599h0M4892 500h0M4897 579h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4897 579h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4897 579h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Castletown. <a href="/atlas?year=1172&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-isles">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
